tially. Table 4. Family history of colorectal cancer and risk of colorectal cancer according to microsatellite instability in the Nurses’ Health Study a First-degree relatives with colorectal cancer log ( HR ) ^ ( SE ) b HR ^ (95% CI) b P -value P heterogeneity c Unweighted model Microsatellite instability (MSI) Non-MSI-high No Ref. 0.02 Yes 0.33 (0.109) 1.40 (1.129–1.729) 0.002 MSI-high No Ref. Yes 0.71 (0.187) 2.04 (1.416–2.949) 0.0001 Model weighted by w (1) d Microsatellite instability (MSI) Non-MSI-high No Ref. 0.05 Yes 0.38 (0.120) 1.47 (1.159–1.859) 0.001 MSI-high No Ref. Yes 0.70 (0.185) 2.01 (1.398–2.888) 0.0002 Model weighted by w (2) e Microsatellite instability (MSI) MSS/MSI-low No Ref. 0.05 Yes 0.30 (0.120) 1.35 (1.068–1.709) 0.01 MSI-high No Ref. Yes 0.63 (0.186) 1.87 (1.299–2.688) 0.008 Open in a new tab Abbreviation: CI, confidence interval; HR, hazard ratio; MSI, microsatellite instability. a For each control, the weight equals 1 for all time periods before the control was censored. For each case with a known subtype, the weight equals 1 for all time periods before diagnosis, and equals the inverse probability of MSI data availability for the period corresponding to the time of diagnosis. If the probability of MSI availability is not available for a case with a known subtype due to lack of data on disease stage, tumor location, tumor differentiation, age at diagnosis or year of diagnosis, the weight equals 1 for the period corresponding to the time of diagnosis. For each case without subtype information, the weight equals 1 for all periods before the time of diagnosis, and equals 0 for the period corresponding to the time of diagnosis. b The HRs were adjusted for body mass index (< 25 vs. 25 – 29.9 vs. ≥ 30 kg/m 2 ), pack-years of smoking (0 vs. 1 – 19 vs. 20 – 39 vs. ≥ 40 pack-years), history of endoscopy (yes/no), physical activity level [quintiles of mean metabolic equivalent task score (METS) - hours per week], total calorie intake (quintiles of