INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7016,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3165578','Epidemiology and evolutionary characteristics of the porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome virus in China between 2006 and 2010.','Li B, Fang L, Guo X, Gao J, Song T, Bi J, He K, Chen H, Xiao S.','2011','broke nsp2 orf5 reemerging nsp2 discontinuous gp5 orf5 prrs sows gilts prrs prrs enveloped orfs orf1a orf1b untranslated nonstructural nsps nsp1α nsp1β nsp2 orf2a orf2b orf3 gp2 gp3 gp4 gp5 lelystad nsp2 orf5 orf5 nsp2 prrs prrs prrs broke prrs discolorations reemerged pork nsp2 orf5 jiangxi minced dulbecco nsp2 tgggcgacaatgtccctaac nsp2 gctgagtattttgggcgtgtg amplifies orf5f tggcaatttgaatgttcaagtatg orf5r ctgtgctatcattgcagaagtcgt orf5 flanking orf4 orf6 nsp2f aggaatgcttggccaaacttgagag nsp2r gctgagtattttgggcgtgtg nsp2 qiaamp hexanucleotide superscript brl pfu deoxynucleoside dntp pfu stratagene cycler reamplify orf5 nsp2 qiaquick qiaquick pmd18 takara amplifications lasergene dnastar dnaman clustalw mega4 nsp2 hq832259 hq832411 orf5 fj870109 fj870124 hq832099 hq832258 eu371943 eu371955 eu399822 eu399870 eu187484 eu262603 nsp2 orf5 subsided broke jiangxi reemerging nsp2 nsp2 nsp2 nsp2 jxhs respprrs nsp2 nsp2 hbye discontinuous hubqj1 discontinuous nsp2 nsp2 u87392 respprrs_mlv af066183 ay032626 ay150312 jxa1 ef112445 hun4 ef635006 jxwn06 ef641008 sy0608 eu144079 dashes nsp2 jxhs respprrs hbye jxa1 hun4 wuh1 jxwn06 nsp2 mega4 orf5 gp5 orf5 orf5 respprrs mlv af066183 ay032626 dq642048 jxa1 ef112445 gp5 mega4 replications gp5 ahaq8 gp5 gp5 gp5 respprrs repro respprrs repro a137s a137f gp5s u87392 respprrs_mlv af066183 ay032626 ay150312 jxa1 ef112445 hun4 ef635006 jxwn06 ef641008 sy0608 eu144079 dashes pne gp5 respprrs repro pnes l39f l39i s37f l41s lvn decoy a29v v27a l28h henxy2 decoy hypervariability nxs gp5 snyx4 hebx6 hubzl432 gp5s jxa1 ahaq8 gp5s prrs prrs nsp2 nsp2 nsp2 nsp2 discontinuous nsp2 hbye hubqj1 hbye nsp2 hubqj1 discontinuous hubqj1 nsp2 eu140613 eu140614 eu140618 nsp2 nsp2 nsp2 gp5 gp5 gp5 gp5 gp5 gp5 prrs mlv reverted pne gp5 l39i l39f l41s ahhu2 jxsc1 jxsc2 s37f sddz5 sddz7 shxwh shxw7 pne respprrs repro jxa1 gp5s gp5 gp5 n44 n44 n44 n44k snyx4 n51 hubzl432 hebx6 n44 n51 ahaq8 ahaq8 orf5 nsp2 ahaq8 boars ahaq8 ahaq8 jxa1 ahaq8 prrs supp_49_9_3175__index 2011aa10a208 2009zx08009 142b allende allende subgenotypes osorio pattnaik gp5 antigenicity barfoed blixenkrone bøtner kamstrup prrs immunodominant nsp2 dispensable mardassi pirzadeh rogan prrs dee orf5 faaberg gp5 faaberg kehrli lagera nsp2 frias staheli isg15 faaberg nonessential nsp2 replicase faaberg keffaber newslett gp5 orf5 lunney benfield murtaugh elam kakach lelystad prrs murtaugh stadejek abrahante nelsen murtaugh faaberg ostrowski nonneutralizing gp5 ectodomain ropp benfield quasispecies kwang nsp2 demographical stadejek oleksiewicz potapchuk podgorska stadejek mega4 unparalleled prrs e526 vigerust wensvoort mystery lelystad mengeling lager vorwald roof yoshii nsp2 nsp2 supp_49_9_3175__index supp_49_9_3175__1 supp_49_9_3175__2 supp_49_9_3175__3 supp_49_9_3175__supplementary_table_1 supp_49_9_3175__supplementary_table_2','Abstract In 2006, an emerging highly pathogenic strain of porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome virus (PRRSV), which causes continuous high fever and a high proportion of deaths in vaccinated pigs of all ages, broke out in mainland China and spread rapidly to neighboring countries. To examine the epidemiology and evolutionary characteristics of Chinese PRRSV after the 2006 outbreak, we tested 2,981 clinical samples collected from 2006 to 2010 in China, determined 153 Nsp2 sequences and 249 ORF5 sequences, and analyzed the epidemiology and genetic diversity of Chinese PRRSV. Our results showed that the percentage of PRRSV-positive specimens collected from sick pigs averaged 60.85% in the past 5 years and that the highly pathogenic PRRSV has become the dominant strain in China. Furthermore, a reemerging strain which apparently evolved from the highly pathogenic PRRSV strain in 2006 appeared to be widely prevalent in China from 2009 onwards. Sequence analyses revealed that the hypervariable region of Nsp2 in most of the isolates contained a discontinuous deletion equivalent to 30 amino acids, along with other types of deletions. Extensive amino acid substitutions in the GP5 sequence translated from ORF5 were found, particularly in the potential neutralization epitope and the N-glycosylation sites. Our results suggest that Chinese PRRSV has undergone rapid evolution and can circumvent immune responses induced by currently used vaccines. Information from this study will help in understanding the evolutionary characteristics of Chinese PRRSV and assist ongoing efforts to develop and use PRRSV vaccines in the future. INTRODUCTION Porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome (PRRS) is recognized as an important disease of swine and is characterized by reproductive failure in sows and gilts or by respiratory disease affecting pigs of all ages. After it was first reported in the United States and Canada in 1987, PRRS has been causing immense economic losses in the swine industry worldwide ( 17 , 22 , 40 ). The etiologic agent, PRRS virus (PRRSV), is a small, enveloped virus with a positive-sense, single-stranded RNA genome approximately 15 kb long ( 35 ). Nine open reading frames (ORFs) have been identified in the PRRSV genome. ORF1a and ORF1b are located downstream of the 5′ untranslated region (UTR) and encode the viral nonstructural proteins (Nsps): Nsp1α, Nsp1β, and Nsp2 to -12 ( 24 , 29 , 30 ). ORF2a, ORF2b, and ORF3 to -7 are located at the 3′ end of the genome and encode the viral structural proteins GP2, E, GP3, GP4, GP5, M, and N, respectively ( 34 ). Historically, PRRSV was classified into two major genotypes, represented by the North American prototype VR-2332 and the European prototype Lelystad virus (LV). The two genotypes exhibit distinct genetic variations, with approximately 60% nucleotide identity at the genome level ( 27 ). Furthermore, strains within each genotype vary considerably, particularly in the Nsp2 and ORF5 genes, with sequence differences of as high as 20% ( 15 , 24 , 27 ). Thus, ORF5 and Nsp2 have become the regions of choice for monitoring the evolution of PRRSV and for molecular epidemiology research on PRRSV ( 7 , 25 , 33 ). In China, PRRS was first reported in 1995 and was encountered in almost all provinces. Since then, PRRS has become one of the most important swine diseases. In May 2006, a so-called porcine high-fever syndrome caused by a highly pathogenic PRRS virus (HP-PRRSV) broke out in the south of China and rapidly spread throughout the country, affecting more than 20 million pigs ( 21 , 37 ). Unlike previous PRRS, this disease was characterized by high and continuous fever, anorexia, red discolorations in the body, and blue ears. The morbidity rate was 50 to 100%, and mortality rate was 20 to 100%. Genomic analyses have shown that HP-PRRSV differs significantly from any other previous isolates (termed classic PRRSV here) ( 21 , 37 ). In 2009, porcine high-fever syndrome reemerged in the central region of China, resulting in increased economic losses to the pork industry. In this study, we investigated the epidemiology of PRRSV from 2006 to 2010 in mainland China and analyzed the evolutionary characteristics of Chinese PRRSV based on the sequences of Nsp2 and ORF5. Our data clearly showed that the percentage of PRRSV-positive specimens collected from sick pigs is high and that this virus is evolving quickly in China. Furthermore, it is possible that some variants have acquired the potential to circumvent immune responses induced by currently used vaccines. MATERIALS AND METHODS Clinical specimens. The clinical samples were collected from different farms in Beijing, Shanghai, and the Hebei, Henan, Hubei, Hunan, Shandong, Anhui, Jiangxi, Jiangsu, Zhejiang, Fujian, Guizhou, Guangdong, and Guangxi provinces of China (see Fig. S1 in the supplemental material). The samples were mainly from sera, lungs, lymph nodes, and brains of diseased pigs that were showing clinical signs such as high fever, anorexia, lethargy, and abdominal breathing. Tissue samples were minced, diluted 1:10 in Dulbecco''s modified Eagle medium (DMEM), homogenized, and centrifuged at 1,500 × g for 10 min to obtain supernatant. All samples were stored at −80°C until reverse transcription-PCR (RT-PCR) testing. Primer design. Three pairs of primers were designed based on a multiple-sequence alignment of the available PRRSV isolates. The primers Nsp2-del-F (5′-TGGGCGACAATGTCCCTAAC-3′) and Nsp2-del-R (5′-GCTGAGTATTTTGGGCGTGTG-3′) were designed for detection with the clinical samples. This primer pair amplifies DNA fragments of different sizes from the classic PRRSV isolate (508 bp) and from the highly pathogenic PRRSV (HP-PRRSV) isolate (418 bp). The primers ORF5F (5′-TGGCAATTTGAATGTTCAAGTATG-3′) and ORF5R (5′-CTGTGCTATCATTGCAGAAGTCGT-3′) were designed to amplify the complete sequence of ORF5 and the flanking regions of ORF4 and ORF6. The primers Nsp2F (5′-AGGAATGCTTGGCCAAACTTGAGAG-3′) and Nsp2R (5′-GCTGAGTATTTTGGGCGTGTG-3′) were designed to amplify the highly variable regions of the Nsp2 gene (about 1100 bp) for sequencing. Preparation of RNA and RT-PCR. Viral RNA was extracted from the clinical samples with the QIAamp viral RNA kit (Qiagen). Virus cDNA was constructed by reverse transcription using random hexanucleotide primers and SuperScript II reverse transcriptase (Gibco BRL) following the supplier''s instructions. The cDNA was used as the template in the subsequent PCR in a final volume of 50 μl that included 25 pmol of each of the PRRSV-specific primers described above, 1× Pfu polymerase buffer, 200 μM deoxynucleoside triphosphate (dNTP), and 2.5 units of Pfu DNA polymerase (Stratagene). The PCR was done under the following conditions in a thermal cycler: 1 cycle of 5 min at 95°C; 35 cycles of denaturation at 94°C for 30 s, annealing at 57°C for 30 s, and elongation at 72°C for 1 min; and 1 cycle of 10 min at 72°C. The amplified products were analyzed by electrophoresis in a 1% agarose gel and visualized under UV light. Nucleotide sequencing and sequence analysis. One or two positive samples of PRRSV from each farm were chosen to reamplify the ORF5 gene and the longer Nsp2 gene for sequence analysis. For sequencing, the PCR products were purified using a QIAquick gel extraction kit (QIAquick) and cloned into pMD18-T vector (TaKaRa). For each amplified gene region, three clones from each of three independent amplifications were sequenced in both directions; thus, nine determinations of the sequence of each region were performed. Sequence data were assembled and analyzed using the software Lasergene (DNAstar) and DNAMAN. Multiple-sequence alignments were done using ClustalW ( 36 ). The phylogenetic tree was constructed by using the neighbor-joining method with MEGA4 ( 36 ). The robustness of the neighbor-joining tree was estimated by bootstrap analysis with 1,000 replicates. Nucleotide sequence accession numbers. The PRRSV nucleotide sequences reported in this paper were submitted to GenBank. For the Nsp2 genes, the GenBank accession numbers are HQ832259 to HQ832411 , and for the ORF5 genes the GenBank accession numbers are FJ870109 to FJ870124 , HQ832099 to HQ832258 , EU371943 to EU371955 , EU399822 to EU399870 , EU187484 , and EU262603 . RESULTS Epidemiology of PRRSV in China from 2006 to 2010. Of the 2,981 clinical samples collected from the different provinces from May 2006 to December 2010, 1,814 (60.85%) were positive for PRRSV by detection of the Nsp2 and ORF5 genes. In 2006, 56.31% (165/293) were positive for HP-PRRSV and 7.85% (23/293) were positive for the classic PRRSV ( Table 1 ). In 2007, 64.86% (408/629) of samples were positive for HP-PRRSV and no classic PRRSV was detected. Similar patterns were observed in 2008, but the PRRSV-positive rate dropped to 55.06%, suggesting that the porcine high-fever syndrome subsided gradually in 2008. However, this epidemic disease broke out again in Anhui province in 2009, and many pig farms in central regions of China, including the Anhui, Hubei, Henan, Shandong, and Jiangsu provinces, suffered from HP-PRRSV infections. Our data also show that the highest rate of HP-PRRSV (71.71%) was observed in this year. In 2010, the results of a large-scale detection effort indicated that the rate of PRRSV infection dropped to 54.03%. Table 1. Rate of PRRSV infection from 2006 to 2010 in China Yr of sample collection No. of isolates a Infection rate (%) Total HP-PRRSV C-PRRSV HP-PRRSV C-PRRSV Total 2006 293 165 23 56.31 7.85 64.16 2007 629 408 0 64.86 0 64.86 2008 603 332 0 55.06 0 55.06 2009 562 403 0 71.71 0 71.71 2010 894 483 0 54.03 0 54.03 Open in a new tab a HP-PRRSV, highly pathogenic PRRSV; C-PRRSV, classical PRRSV. In addition, the infection of different geographical region was further analyzed. There was a very high infection rate in Jiangxi province (approximately 81.0%) in 2006, which was the first HP-PRRSV outbreak area. Similarly, the rate of infection with reemerging HP-PRRSV in Anhui province was 76.64% in 2009. The other regions with high rates (more than 68.0%) of HP-PRRSV were Hubei and Henan provinces. Apart from these four provinces, the percentage of PRRSV-positive specimens collected from sick pigs was between 53.5% and 65.0% since 2006. Genetic diversity of Nsp2 in PRRSV isolates. To better understand the evolutionary characteristics of PRRSV, a 1,100-bp DNA fragment within the Nsp2 gene from 153 positive samples (from 2006 to 2010) (see Table S1 in the supplemental material) was amplified, cloned, and sequenced. Sequence analysis showed that all 153 Nsp2 genes of the PRRSV isolates shared 89.5 to 99.5% sequence identity with VR-2332, indicating that these isolates belonged to the North American genotype. By comparing the translated Nsp2 amino acid sequences of the 153 PRRSV isolates, we found that the JXHS strain (isolated in 2006) was the most closely related to the RespPRRS vaccine and its parent VR-2332. Extensive mutations were observed in the Nsp2 gene (a representative alignment is shown in Fig. 1 ; for a detailed alignment, see Fig. S2 in the supplemental material), but there were no recurring mutations or substitutions. Notably, three different types of deletions were seen in the Nsp2 sequences ( Fig. 1 ). Compared to strain VR-2332, strain HBYE (isolated in 2006) contains one amino acid deletion at position 481. All the isolates, isolated from 2006 to 2010, contain discontinuous deletions of 1 and 29 amino acids (aa) at positions 481 and 533 to 561, except for strain HUBQJ1-2 (isolated in 2009), which contains a discontinuous 59-amino-acid deletion of 30 and 29 amino acids at positions 471 to 500 and 533 to 561, respectively. No insertions could be found in the Nsp2 sequences that were examined. Fig. 1. Open in a new tab Alignment of the deduced partial Nsp2 amino acid sequences of 23 representative clinical PRRSV isolates. The sequences of VR-2332 ( U87392 ), RespPRRS_MLV( AF066183 ), CH-1a ( AY032626 ), HB-1(sh)/2002 ( AY150312 ), JXA1( EF112445 ), HUN4 ( EF635006 ), JXwn06 ( EF641008 ), and SY0608 ( EU144079 ) were also used for comparison. Dashes indicate positions where the amino acid exactly matches the reference sequence of VR-2332. The deletion regions are highlighted by gray. To investigate the genetic relationship of the 153 PRRSV isolates with other representative PRRSV isolates, phylogenetic trees were constructed by the neighbor-joining method based on the partial amino acids sequences of Nsp2 ( Fig. 2 ). The phylogenetic analysis showed that the 153 PRRSV isolates could be divided into three major branches. Strain JXHS was closely related to the RespPRRS vaccine, and HBYE shared a close relationship with HB-1(sh)/2002 and clustered between the classic PRRSV (CH-1a) and HP-PRRSV. The other 151 isolates all formed a large cluster and were highly homologous to JXA1, HUN4, WUH1, and JXwn06, which were representative HP-PRRSV isolates ( 18 , 37 , 44 ). They also were highly homologous to each other. Fig. 2. Open in a new tab Phylogenetic tree of the clinical PRRSV isolates based on their translated partial Nsp2 amino acid sequences. The phylogenetic tree was generated by the distance-based neighbor-joining method using MEGA4 software. Bootstrap values were calculated from 1,000 replicates of the alignment. The different subgroups are marked. Classification and phylogenetic analysis of the ORF5 gene. To study the evolution of PRRSV based on the immunological significance and polymorphic nature of GP5 during the last 5 years, we determined the sequences of 249 ORF5 genes from clinical samples collected between 2006 and 2010 (see Table S2 in the supplemental material). Phylogenetic analysis using the translated sequences of the 249 sequenced samples and the sequences of representative PRRSV isolates previously deposited in the database indicated that our isolates clustered in four subgroups, which we termed subgroups 1, 2, 3, and 4. Among the 249 ORF5 sequences, 4 isolates (1.6%) belonged to subgroup 1 represented by RespPRRS MLV (accession number AF066183 ), 4 (1.6%) belonged to subgroup 2 represented by CH-1a ( AY032626 ), 23 (9.2%) belonged to subgroup 3 represented by HB-1(sh)/2002 ( DQ642048 ), and 218 (87.6%) belonged to subgroup 4 represented by JXA1 ( EF112445 ) ( Fig. 3 ). Fig. 3. Open in a new tab Phylogenetic analysis of the translated PRRSV GP5 amino acid sequences of representative PRRSV strains by the distance-based neighbor-joining method using MEGA4 software. The robustness of the tree was assessed by bootstrap analysis with 1,000 replications. The four Chinese subgroups are marked. Based on the phylogenetic tree of GP5, we noticed that the isolates in 2009 to 2010 formed a novel minor branch of subgroup 4 that shared a closer relationship with AHAQ8 (2009), which may have been the initial strain of this epidemic disease in 2009 ( Fig. 3 ). This minor branch included almost all the isolates from 2009 to 2010, indicating that they shared a close relationship with each other and were evolutionarily distant from the pre-2009 isolates. Sequence comparison and analysis of the main amino acid mutations in GP5. The 249 GP5 sequences share 85.5 to 100% amino acid sequence identity. Most of the amino acid substitutions are located in two hypervariable regions at the amino-terminal region (amino acid positions 1 to 25 [signal sequence] and 29 to 39) (a representative alignment is shown in Fig. 4 ; for a detailed alignment, see Fig. S3 in the supplemental material). Residues 13 and 151 of GP5 are believed to be associated with the virulence of PRRSV ( 1 , 41 ). It has been shown that R 13 and R 151 are altered to Q 13 and G 151 when VR-2332 is attenuated to the RespPRRS/Repro vaccine strain ( 1 ). In subgroup 1, all the isolates had residues Q 13 and G 151 and were identical to the attenuated RespPRRS/Repro vaccine strain. In addition, residue A 137 , which was assumed to differentiate the vaccine strains ( 41 ), was present in all isolates of subgroup 1. Nearly all isolates in subgroups 2, 3, and 4 had both the R 13 and R 151 residues, except in subgroup 4, two isolates had a G 13 residue and five isolates had a K 151 residue. In addition, the A137S mutation was present in all isolates of subgroups 2, 3, and 4, except for four isolates in subgroup 4 that exhibited a novel A137F substitution that has not been reported in previous studies. Fig. 4. Open in a new tab Alignment of the deduced amino acid sequences of GP5s from representative clinical PRRSV isolates. The sequences of VR-2332 ( U87392 ), RespPRRS_MLV( AF066183 ), CH-1a ( AY032626 ), HB-1(sh)/2002 ( AY150312 ), JXA1( EF112445 ), HUN4 ( EF635006 ), JXwn06 ( EF641008 ), and SY0608 ( EU144079 ) were also used for comparison. The dashed box indicates the putative signal sequence. The solid box indicates the hypervariable domains. The main amino acid mutations within different subgroups are highlighted by gray. A novel substitution is marked with a star. Dashes indicate positions where the amino acid exactly matches the consensus sequence. High variability in the primary neutralizing epitope (PNE) (aa 37 to 44) of GP5 has been reported in a previous study ( 28 ). Compared with the attenuated RespPRRS/Repro vaccine strain, no mutation could be found in the PNEs of isolates within subgroup 1, but an L39F amino acid mutation was observed within subgroup 2. Interestingly, all isolates within subgroups 3 and 4 had an L39I mutation ( Fig. 4 ; see Fig. S3 in the supplemental material). In addition, the mutations at S 37 (S37F) and L 41 (L41S) were found in a few isolates. The epitope V 27 LVN may function as a decoy epitope causing a diminished immune responsiveness against an adjacent primary neutralizing epitope ( 28 ). All the sequences in subgroup 2 and most of sequences in subgroups 3 and 4 contain the A29V mutation, the V27A and L28H mutations occur in subgroup 3, and strain HeNXY2 contains all three mutations simultaneously. It is worth noting that there is no mutation at the decoy epitope in the novel minor branch of subgroup 4 formed in 2009 to 2010. Hypervariability of potential N-glycosylation sites (identified by the sequence NXS/T, where X is any amino acid except proline) was also observed in the 249 GP5 sequences (see Fig. S3 in the supplemental material). The potential N-glycosylation sites at amino acid positions 44 and 51 were mostly conserved within the four subgroups, except at residue 44 of isolate SNYX4 and at residues 51 of isolates HEBX6 and HUBZL432. In contrast, the potential N-glycosylation sites between positions 30 and 35 varied. The potential N-glycosylation site was at position 32 in 4 isolates and at position 33 in 1 of the isolates in subgroup 2. In subgroups 3 and 4, there were potential N-glycosylation sites at positions 30, 33, 34, and 35 simultaneously ( Table 2 ). Thus, different numbers of potential N-glycosylation sites were detected in the GP5s from different isolates ( Table 3 ). Interestingly, all isolates within subgroup 1 and most isolates within subgroup 2 contained 3 potential N-glycosylation sites, while most isolates within subgroups 3 and 4 possessed 4 or 5 potential N-glycosylation sites. In subgroup 4, there were 5 potential N-glycosylation sites in the isolates, represented by JXA1, and 4 potential N-glycosylation sites in the novel-branch isolates, represented by AHAQ8. Table 2. Distribution of amino acid positions in the potential N-glycosylation sites Subgroup No. of isolates with potential N-glycosylation site at the indicated positions/total no. of isolates 30–32 32 a –34 33–35 34–36 35–37 44–46 51–53 1 4/4 0/4 0/4 0/4 0/4 4/4 4/4 2 0/4 4/4 1/4 0/4 0/4 4/4 4/4 3 15/23 1/23 3/23 17/23 13/23 23/23 23/23 4 205/218 0/218 5/218 109/218 199/218 217/218 216/218 Open in a new tab a Novel N-glycosylation site position which was not reported in previous studies. Table 3. Number of potential N-glycosylation sites in different deduced GP5s Subgroup No. of isolates with the indicated no. of potential N-glycosylation sites/total no. of isolates 3 4 5 1 4/4 0/4 0/4 2 3/4 1/4 0/4 3 7/23 8/23 8/23 4 12/218 119/218 87/218 Open in a new tab DISCUSSION PRRS continues to be a serious threat, causing an economically significant impact on the swine industry worldwide ( 22 , 32 ). Although commercial vaccines against PRRSV are available, traditional control strategies and the conventional vaccines have failed to provide sustainable disease control. Since the first report in 1995, PRRS has become an increasing threat in China. In particular, the devastating outbreak of HP-PRRSV in 2006 affected more than 2,000,000 pigs, with about 400,000 fatal cases ( 21 , 37 ). To examine the epidemiology and genetic characteristics of Chinese PRRSV during the past 5 years, here we first investigated the percentage of PRRSV-positive specimens collected from sick pigs in China from 2006 to 2010. Based on the results from 2,981 clinical samples collected from different provinces, the average positive rate for PRRSV was 60.85% (1,814/2,981). The positive rates in the different years were 64.16% in 2006, 64.86% in 2007, 55.06% in 2008, 71.71% in 2009, and 54.03% in 2010, indicating that the PRRSV infection was sustained at a high level over the 5 years. Interestingly, in 2006, 56.31% (165/293) of the infections were with the highly pathogenic PRRSV and 7.85% (23/293) were with the classic PRRSV ( Table 1 ). From 2007 to 2010, however, no classic PRRSV was detected in any of the samples we received, suggesting that the highly pathogenic PRRSV is now the dominant PRRSV in China. PRRSV undergoes remarkable genetic alterations. The Nsp2 gene has the highest genetic diversity in the genome of PRRSV ( 2 , 27 ). It is, therefore, one of the main targets used to monitor PRRSV evolution ( 25 ). Furthermore, the Nsp2-coding region is crucial for viral replication and modulation of host immunity because of its protease activity ( 2 , 8 , 13 , 42 ). Natural insertions and/or deletions are routinely observed in the hypervariable middle region of the Nsp2 protein in field strains, resulting in polymorphisms of considerable size ( 16 , 31 ). Among the 153 Nsp2 sequences that we examined, we found three different deletions. However, most of the isolates contain discontinuous deletions of 1 and 29 amino acids at positions 481 and 533 to 561 in Nsp2. Although the 30-amino-acid deletion is not associated with the virulence of the emerging HP-PRRSV ( 44 ), it could be used as an epidemiological genetic marker for the dominant PRRSV in China since 2006. Two other types of deletion were found in strains HBYE and HUBQJ1-2. Strain HBYE has only 1 amino acid deletion at position 481 in the amino acid sequence of Nsp2. A similar single deletion has been observed in other Chinese PRRSV isolates, leading to the suggestion that the isolates possessing this one-amino-acid deletion are an intermediate virus in the evolution of the HP-PRRSV ( 3 , 18 , 43 ). Strain HUBQJ1-2 contains a discontinuous 59-amino-acid deletion comprising deletions of 30 and 29 amino acids at positions 471 to 500 and 533 to 561, respectively. The HUBQJ1-2 sample was collected in Hubei province in 2009. Interestingly, a similar deletion in Nsp2 was observed in isolates from Guizhou province in 2007 (GenBank accession numbers EU140613 , EU140614 , and EU140618 ), implying that these isolates may have been prevalent in pigs in China. However, it is possible that these isolates were never widespread because the large deletions may affect the ability of the virus to survive ( 12 ). In addition to the deletions mentioned above, other types of deletions, i.e., of 12 amino acids, 68 amino acids, 65 amino acids, 51 amino acids, and 131 amino acids, have been observed in other isolates in previous studies ( 14 , 16 , 19 , 29 , 45 ). It is interesting that the deletions in the viral genomes of field PRRSV isolates occur mostly in the Nsp2 region. Further study should be done to elucidate the putative mechanism for the observed frequent deletion in Nsp2. In addition to study of mutations and deletions in Nsp2, the genetic diversity of GP5 has received much attention because GP5 is the most important immunogenic protein and contains the primary neutralization epitope of PRRSV ( 6 , 20 , 39 ). Furthermore, GP5 is one of the most genetically variable structural proteins of PRRSV. Thus, GP5 has also been a target for analysis of the genetic diversity of PRRSV ( 4 , 9 , 10 , 23 ). Our study demonstrated that all 249 Chinese PRRSV isolates belonged to the North American genotype and could be further divided into four subgroups based on the amino acid sequences of GP5. From our analysis of residues 13 and 151 of GP5, we concluded that the subgroup 1 isolates are probably related to the extensive use of the attenuated modified live PRRS vaccine (MLV). Some isolates may have reverted to a pathogenic phenotype, as described in previous studies ( 4 , 39 ). The subgroup 2 and 3 isolates are most closely related to the classic PRRSV and may be the source of the highly pathogenic PRRSV (CH-1a-like PRRSV) and the intermediate virus in PRRSV evolution, respectively. The subgroup 4 isolates were most closely related to the currently dominant HP-PRRSV. Our study revealed that extensive mutations had occurred in the identified primary neutralizing epitope (PNE) (amino acids 37 to 44) of GP5 ( 28 ). Mutations L39I and L39F were observed in subgroups 3 and 4 and in subgroup 2, respectively. In addition, unique mutations identified in recent isolates included L41S in 3 isolates (AHHU2, JXSC1, and JXSC2) and S37F in 4 isolates (SDDZ5, SDDZ7, SHXWH, and SHXW7). The substitutions of these critical amino acids in PNE might have allowed these field isolates to escape neutralization by the antibodies induced by the attenuated RespPRRS/Repro vaccine strain, the CH-1R vaccine strain (an attenuated vaccine strain derived from CH-1a, used in China), and the attenuated live vaccine based on HP-PRRSV (an attenuated vaccine strain derived from JXA1, used from 2009 in China), resulting in incomplete immune protection. Differences at the potential N-glycosylation sites, including their distribution and quality, were also observed in the GP5s of different isolates. The number of the potential N-glycosylation sites apparently increased in the Chinese isolates over time. Most isolates in subgroup 4 possessed five potential N-glycosylation sites ( Table 3 ). Additional N-glycosylation sites may lead to an increase in the number of the N-linked glycans, providing a barrier to antibody evasion which, in this case, does not affect the ability of the virus to bind to cellular receptors in the host ( 11 ). The evolving glycan shield, therefore, presents a possible mechanism for viral persistence despite an increasing antibody repertoire ( 20 , 38 ). A previous study has shown that when the N-glycosylation sites of GP5 are abolished, both the sensitivity of the mutant virus to in vitro neutralization and the immunogenicity of the nearby neutralization epitope are enhanced ( 6 ). The increasing number of N-glycosylation sites in GP5 therefore further supports our hypothesis that field isolates appear to possess the capability to escape the neutralization antibodies induced by current vaccines. Ansari et al. demonstrated that mutations of residue N44 resulted in progeny that was noninfectious ( 5 ), indicating that N44 is the most critical amino acid residue for PRRSV infectivity. However, we found a mutation in the N44 glycosylation site (N44K) in PRRSV SNYX4 isolated in 2010. A similar mutation has been observed in a previous study ( 39 ). The mutation of N51 that we found in HUBZL432 in 2009 and in HEBX6 in 2010 has not been reported earlier. The biological characterization of the N44 and N51 deletion isolates will be investigated in future work. Another interesting finding from this study is the novel minor branch within subgroup 4 formed by a representative isolate, AHAQ8 (2009), and other isolates from 2009 to 2010. AHAQ8 (2009) was collected during the outbreak of PRRSV in Anhui province in 2009. Isolates with ORF5 and Nsp2 gene sequences identical to those of AHAQ8 (2009) were observed in the neighboring provinces of Hubei and Henan. Furthermore, the 2009 outbreak spread rapidly from Anhui province to Hubei and Henan provinces. A retrospective survey found that several groups of piglets and boars had been moved from Anhui province to Hubei and Henan provinces during the 2009 outbreak. It has been speculated that the large-scale movement of pigs was a major reason for the spread of PRRSV in these provinces in 2009. Thus, pig transport should be prohibited during a PRRSV outbreak. In addition, we found many isolates similar to AHAQ8 (2009) in more and more provinces in China in 2010, and these formed the novel minor branch. Considering the genetic diversity of AHAQ8 (2009) and JXA1, the representative strain of HP-PRRSV that emerged in 2006, we conclude that AHAQ8 (2009) is an emerging strain now prevalent in China. Heterogeneity is considered to be a major obstacle for the effective prevention and control of PRRS ( 24 , 26 ). Chinese PRRSV has undergone rapid evolution in recent years. The high degree of genetic and antigenic diversity among field isolates that we observed underlines the complexity of the control and eradication of the disease in China. An understanding of all of these factors will be crucial in planning the development and use of vaccines against PRRSV in the future. Supplementary Material [Supplemental material] supp_49_9_3175__index.html (1.9KB, html) ACKNOWLEDGMENTS This work was supported by the 863 project (2011AA10A208), the Genetically Modified Organisms Breeding Major Projects (2009ZX08009-142B), and the National Natural Sciences Foundation of China (31001066, 30972189). Footnotes † Supplemental material for this article may be found at http://jcm.asm.org/ . ▿ Published ahead of print on 20 July 2011. REFERENCES 1. Allende R., et al. 2000. Mutations in the genome of porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome virus responsible for the attenuation phenotype. Arch. Virol. 145: 1149–1161 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Allende R., et al. 1999. North American and European porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome viruses differ in non-structural protein coding regions. J. Gen. Virol. 80: 307–315 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. An T. Q., et al. 2010. Origin of highly pathogenic porcine reproductive and respiratory syndrome virus, China. Emerg. Infect. Dis. 16: 365–367 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 4. An T. Q., et al. 2007. Genetic diversity and phylogenetic analysis of glycoprotein 5 of PRRSV isolates in mainland China from 1