lar ] 20. Hyodo I, Eguchi K, Nishina T, Endo H, Tanimizu M, Mikami I, Takashima S, Imanishi J. Perceptions and attitudes of clinical oncologists on complementary and alternative medicine. Cancer. 2003;97(11):2861–2868. doi: 10.1002/cncr.11402. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Dantas F, Rampes H. Do homeopathic medicines provoke adverse effects? A systematic review. Br Hom J. 2000;89:35–38. doi: 10.1054/homp.1999.0378. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Fønnebø V, Grimsgaard S, Walach H, Ritenbaugh C, Norheim AJ, MacPherson H, Lewith G, Launsø L, Koithan M, Falkenberg T, et al. Researching complementary and alternative treatments - the gatekeepers are not at home. BMC Med Res Methodol. 2007;7(7):1–6. doi: 10.1186/1471-2288-7-7. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Cassileth BR, Lusk EJ, Strouse TB, Bodenheimer BJ. Contemporary unorthodox treatment in cancer medicine. Ann Intern Med. 1984;101:105–112. doi: 10.7326/0003-4819-101-1-105. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Salamonsen A. Doctor-patient communication and cancer patient''s choice of alternative therapies as supplement or alternative to conventional care. Scand J Caring Sci. 2013;27(1):70–76. doi: 10.1111/j.1471-6712.2012.01002.x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Bordens KS, Abbott BB: Research design and methods. A process approach, fifth edition edn. Boston: McGraw-hill High Educ; 2002. 26. Kristoffersen A, Fonnebo V, Norheim A. Do cancer patients with a poor prognosis use complementary and alternative medicine more often than others? J Altern Complement Med. 2009;15(1):35–40. doi: 10.1089/acm.2008.0262. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Frenkel M, Ben-Arye E, Cohen L. Communication in cancer care: discussing complementary and alternative medicine. Integr Cancer Ther. 2010;9(2):177–185. doi: 10.1177/1534735410363706. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Halbesleben JRB, Whitman MV. Evaluating survey quality in health services research: a