INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(7062,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12167241','Utilization of complementary, alternative, and integrative medicine practices among US adults with and without a diagnosis of cancer.','Nemesure B, Wood SK, Mermelstein L.','2025','meditating endure psychosocially botanicals disproportional acupuncturist ged slept slept meditating breathwork mbis mbis mbis mbis mbis gentler dineen pamela ginex clare moyer chitra ioana zervos stony giaquinto nekhlyudov mollica dhawan fajardo phutrakool pongpirul zelinski tejedor casado heslop sabesan sapire lacey sierpina gowin muminovic zick e431554 hilfiker meichtry eicher mediational beuken hochstenbach copenhaver hempel sanford sher jazieh abuelgasim ardah belloni arrigoni xunlin klainin yobas pascoe ski shergill khoo khoo kreutz steindorf danon gobari burnand rodondi oberoi woodgate e2012598 e2012598 cillessen johannsen speckens zachariae tarver secinti yoga4cancer yoga4cancer mccombie lacchetti alici physician_gls kanherkar stair giridharan','Abstract Purpose The purpose of this study was to compare the utilization of complementary, alternative, and integrative medicine (CAIM) therapies between US adults with and without cancer. Methods This investigation is based on publicly available data from the 2022 National Health Information Survey (NHIS). Descriptive statistics are presented for demographic characteristics, physical and mental health factors, and CAIM practices stratified by cancer status. Differences between groups were assessed using chi-squared tests, with significance defined by p < 0.05. Results The study included 24,184 individuals without cancer and 3430 individuals with a self-reported history of any cancer type. The majority of participants were of non-Hispanic White racial-ethnic background, 54% were female, and those with cancer were found to be significantly older than those without. Approximately 18% of adults reported meditating during the past 12 months, and 15% practiced yoga; however, individuals with a history of cancer were significantly less likely to practice yoga than non-cases ( p < 0.001). While cancer cases sought out practices for pain relief more frequently than non-cases, the utilization of these activities was < 5%. Conclusions Notably, fewer than 20% of US adults who completed the NHIS 2022 survey, regardless of cancer status, reported participating in CAIM practices within the prior year. While some may not find CAIM therapies beneficial or of interest, providing education to this population has the potential to better aid with symptom management. Future research should explore influencing factors for the adoption of these practices to quantify the impact of these modalities and uncover the potential epigenetic changes and biological mechanisms responsible for their effects, particularly among those diagnosed with cancer. Keywords: Complementary medicine, Alternative medicine, Integrative medicine, Cancer, Cancer survivor, Utilization Introduction Cancer remains one of the leading causes of mortality worldwide and the second leading cause of death in the United States (US) [ 1 ]. While early prevention and new treatments have improved survival rates, there are considerable sequelae that cancer survivors endure psychosocially and biologically [ 2 ]. Complementary and alternative medicine (CAM) [ 3 ], also more recently operationalized as complementary, alternative, and integrative medicine (CAIM) [ 4 ], has been used for centuries worldwide to improve physical, mental, and spiritual wellness [ 4 ]. By definition, complementary medicine involves using a non-traditional medical intervention along with conventional medical therapy, whereas alternative medicine involves therapies used in place of conventional medical therapy. Integrative medicine brings complementary and conventional medical interventions together in a holistic approach to healthcare [ 4 ]. Although the Western world has been slow to adopt CAIM practices to aid in healing and assist with restoring overall health[ 5 ], utilization of these non-pharmaceutical approaches has marginally increased in the US over the past decade [ 6 ]. In the general population, non-conventional healthcare approaches have been used to manage chronic pain, reduce blood pressure, symptoms of anxiety and depression, or insomnia [ 7 ]. In the cancer domain, CAIM practices have been implemented to relieve symptoms such as nausea–vomiting, anorexia, anxiety, depression, constipation, and sleep disturbances [ 8 ], as well as to improve general health, reduce side effects of medical treatments, exert control in the management of cancer and its symptoms, reduce emotional distress, and improve energy [ 9 ]. CAIM use in patients with cancer has also been adopted in the palliative care setting to provide essential therapeutic components of supportive care, addressing complex needs not being met by conventional medicine through the cancer survivorship trajectory and during the end of life [ 10 ]. Activities such as yoga, meditation, guided imagery, progressive muscle relaxation, and other similar practices have been shown to be effective at reducing pain, decreasing stress, facilitating relaxation, optimizing sleep, and enhancing quality of life [ 8 ]. Additionally, alternative therapies in oncology have been reportedly used in place of traditional oncologic treatments. These may include (but are not limited to) biologically based therapies such as diet, vitamins, and botanicals, energy therapies, mind–body practices, manipulative body practices such as chiropractic therapy, and cultural healing practices such as naturopathic medicine, as well as Chinese medicine and Ayurvedic medicine [ 7 ]. With the growing interest in non-Western and holistic approaches to the treatment of cancer, integrative oncology is becoming an emerging subspecialty in care akin to integrative medicine, which provides a more holistic approach that recognizes the value of mind–body practices alongside conventional treatment [ 11 ]. The National Center for Complementary and Alternative Medicine, now known as the National Center for Complementary and Integrative Health, was established in 1998 with a mission to ensure the usefulness and safety of complementary and integrative interventions through rigorous scientific research [ 7 ]. As such, a growing evidence base of cancer research continues to investigate the implementation of these CAIM practices for symptom management of cancer treatment sequelae and to improve quality of life [ 8 , 12 – 14 ]. Cancer-related fatigue is one of the most pervasive symptoms experienced by cancer survivors, reported by over 50% during and after treatment [ 15 , 16 ]. Recent reports indicate that approximately 40–55% of individuals with cancer who undergo curative treatment report having pain; similarly 40–55% experience pain after cancer treatment, and 66% of those with advanced disease suffer from significant levels of pain [ 17 , 18 ]. Managing pain, along with its concomitant conditions such as stress, fatigue, anxiety, and depression, is challenging. While research exists for the benefits of diverse mind–body interventions on symptom management and general well-being among individuals with cancer [ 19 ], there is much heterogeneity in these findings. In addition, a greater proportion of this research is among individuals with breast cancer, which may not be generalizable to all patients with a diagnosis of cancer [ 8 ]. Furthermore, there is a variability of existing data for the extent to which cancer survivors are adopting such approaches [ 9 , 20 – 22 ]. Given that CAIM therapies may improve symptom management, particularly for oncology populations, it is important to understand characteristics among individuals who do and do not engage in these practices. In addition, exploring differences in utilization of CAIM between the general and oncology populations may provide key information for unique needs, preferences, and benefits of CAIM between these populations that can inform optimal types of CAIM therapies and education specific to individualized needs. Therefore, the purpose of this investigation was to compare the utilization of CAIM practices among adults in the US with and without cancer. Individuals with a personal history of cancer are known to face disproportional levels of pain, anxiety, depression, and fatigue compared to the general population, and the findings from this work may help to guide targeted initiatives for integrating CAIM into oncology healthcare practices, thereby improving the overall quality of life for individuals with cancer. Methods The National Health Information Survey (NHIS) was implemented in 1957 through the Centers for Disease Control (CDC) and is designed to monitor the health of the US population through questionnaires that ascertain demographic and health-related information throughout the nation [ 23 ]. Data are collected for both children and adults and are used to monitor illness trends and to evaluate progress in achieving national health goals. Since the information is publicly available, researchers also utilize these datasets for epidemiologic evaluations and other types of investigations. The NHIS represents a cross-sectional, geographically, and nationally representative cluster sample of households. While the main instrument surveys collect responses to standardized questions repeated over time, supplemental questions are sometimes included during a given cycle to acquire additional information in a particular domain. In 2022, the questionnaire included a set of additional questions related to CAIM practices. The items were designed to collect information related to the use of CAIM therapies for both pain and restorative health, which included seeing or speaking with a chiropractor, acupuncturist, massage therapist, naturopathic doctor, or art or music therapist. Information regarding practices of meditation, guided imagery or progressive muscle relaxation, and yoga was also ascertained. The present investigation evaluated the utilization of these practices among survey participants during the past 12 months. Statistical analyses Descriptive statistics are presented for demographic characteristics, emotional and physical factors, and CAIM practices for the sample. The data were stratified by cancer status, a dichotomous variable defined by a self-reported response to the question, “Have you ever been told that you have cancer?” Since it was not known whether those who replied “yes” to this question were actively undergoing treatment for cancer or had completed treatment, we refer to this group hereafter as “individuals with cancer”. Participants with missing data for this question were excluded from the study. Demographic characteristics included age, gender, race, ethnicity, education level, marital status, and health insurance status. Emotional and physical factors included the extent of anxiety and depression (e.g., daily, weekly, monthly, a few times a year, or never), as well as medication use and utilization of therapy services for mental health purposes. Level of tiredness (e.g., never, some days, most days, or every day) and the duration of daily sleep (number of hours per night), as well as the extent of weekly physical aerobic and strength training activities (number of times per week), were also captured in the survey. χ 2 tests were used to evaluate differences between groups. p -values < 0.05 were considered to be statistically significant. SPSS, version 21, was used to carry out the analyses. Results This study included 3430 cases with a self-reported history of (any) cancer and 24,184 individuals who indicated that they had never been told that they had cancer (non-cases). Table 1 presents the demographic characteristics of the participants stratified by cancer status. Findings revealed that individuals with cancer were significantly older than non-cases ( p < 0.01). The overall sample included more females than males, and the racial–ethnic distribution of both groups included predominantly non-Hispanic White participants. Approximately 50% of the sample had at least an associate’s degree, just under half were married, and more than 90% had health insurance. Hispanic participants ( n = 3943) were less likely than non-Hispanic participants to use CAIM practices ( p < 0.001). Among those Hispanic participants with a history of cancer ( n = 173), 42.8% reported using at least one form of CAIM, while only 29.8% without a history of cancer reported using any form of CAIM ( p < 0.001). The χ 2 statistics comparing differences between groups were highly significant ( p < 0.001) for all characteristics presented in Table 1 except body mass index ( χ 2 = 4.8, p = 0.2). Table 1. Demographic characteristics of NHIS (2022) participants stratified by cancer status No cancer Cancer Total N = 24,184 N = 3430 N = 27,614 Characteristic N (%) N (%) N (%) Age, years 18–49 11,610 (48.1) 296 (8.6) 11,906 (43.2) 50–64 6114 (25.3) 780 (22.8) 6894 (25.0) 65 + 6405 (26.5) 2347 (68.6) 8752 (31.8) Gender Male 11,130 (46.0) 1449 (42.2) 12,579 (45.6) Female 13,051 (54.0) 1981 (57.8) 15,032 (54.4) Race White 17,378 (76.1) 3070 (90.9) 20,448 (78.1) Black 3031 (13.3) 195 (5.8) 3226 (11.7) Asian 1631 (7.1) 52 (1.5) 1683 (6.1) Other 781 (3.4) 59 (1.7) 840 (3.0) Ethnicity Hispanic 3770 (15.6) 173 (5.0) 3943 (14.3) Non-Hispanic 20,414 (84.4) 3257 (95.0) 23,671 (85.7) Education ≤ 12 (no diploma) 2117 (8.8) 247 (7.2) 2364 (8.6) GED/High School graduate 6174 (25.7) 802 (23.5) 6976 (25.3) Some college (no degree) 3592 (14.9) 511 (14.9) 4103 (14.9) Associate’s degree 3134 (13.0) 491 (14.4) 3625 (13.1) Bachelor’s degree 5554 (23.1) 746 (21.8) 6300 (22.8) Master’s degree or higher 3477 (14.5) 622 (18.2) 4099 (14.8) Marital status Single 6484 (28.1) 314 (9.5) 6798 (25.7) Married 10,675 (46.3) 1621 (48.8) 12,296 (46.6) Widowed 2067 (9.0) 685 (20.6) 2752 (10.4) Divorced 3470 (15.0) 664 (20.0) 4134 (15.7) Separated 384 (1.7) 37 (1.1) 421 (1.6) Health insurance Yes 22,075 (91.6) 3376 (98.5) 25,451 (92.4) No 2032 (8.4) 50 (1.5) 2082 (7.6) General health status Excellent 5243 (21.7) 415 (12.1) 5658 (20.5) Very good 8451 (34.9) 1008 (29.4) 9459 (34.3) Good 7143 (29.5) 1107 (32.3) 8250 (29.9) Fair 2605 (10.8) 610 (17.8) 3215 (11.6) Poor 739 (3.1) 288 (8.4) 1027 (3.7) Body mass index Underweight 364 (1.5) 68 (2.0) 432 (1.6) Healthy weight 7442 (31.5) 1,41 (31.0) 8483 (31.4) Overweight 8111 (34.3) 1163 (34.7) 9274 (34.4) Obese 7723 (32.7) 1081 (32.2) 8804 (32.6) History of comorbidities Diabetes 2382 (9.9) 564 (16.4) 2946 (10.7) Hypertension 8197 (33.9) 1946 (56.8) 10,143 (36.8) Hypercholesterolemia 6972 (28.9) 1746 (51.1) 8718 (31.7) Open in a new tab Table 2 provides the physical and emotional characteristics of the participants stratified by cancer status. While those without cancer indicated that they felt worried, nervous, or anxious more frequently than those with cancer, a significantly larger percentage of cancer cases reported taking medication for anxiety (17.4% vs. 14.2%, p < 0.001). A higher percentage of individuals with cancer also reported feeling depressed on a daily basis and were more likely to take medication for depression than those without cancer (15.9% vs. 11.9%, p < 0.001). Table 2. Physical/emotional characteristics of NHIS (2022) participants stratified by cancer status No cancer Cancer N = 24,184 N = 3430 χ 2 p -value Characteristic N (%) N (%) Anxiety 44.144 < 0.001 Daily 3170 (13.3) 452 (13.4) Weekly 3564 (15.0) 404 (12.0) Monthly 2808 (11.8) 323 (9.6) A few times a year 7628 (32.1) 1171 (34.8) Never 6578 (27.7) 1018 (30.2) Depression 22.713 < 0.001 Daily 1042 (4.4) 208 (6.2) Weekly 1482 (6.3) 195 (5.8) Monthly 1839 (7.8) 241 (7.1) A few times a year 6902 (29.1) 975 (28.9) Never 12,433 (52.5) 1754 (52.0) Tired 54.944 < 0.001 Every day 921 (3.9) 214 (6.4) Most days 2056 (8.7) 317 (9.4) Some days 12,623 (53.5) 1657 (49.2) Never 8008 (33.9) 1178 (35.0) Moderate physical aerobic activity (number of times/week) 135.317 < 0.001 Never 6569 (28.0) 1094 (32.8) < 1–2 5531 (23.6) 606 (18.1) 3–5 6080 (26.0) 787 (23.6) 6 + 5131 (21.9) 798 (23.9) Unable 110 (0.5) 54 (1.6) Strength training activity (number of times/week) 179.058 < 0.001 Never 13,907 (59.4) 2301 (68.7) < 1–2 4064 (17.4) 359 (10.7) 3–5 3690 (15.8) 426 (12.7) 6 + 1623 (6.9) 213 (6.4) Unable 127 (0.5) 50 (1.5) Sleep (number of hours/night) 73.676 < 0.001 ≤ 4 654 (2.8) 109 (3.3) 5–8 20,676 (88.7) 2793 (83.8) 9 + 1985 (8.5) 432 (13.0) Open in a new tab Regarding physical activity, 32.8% of individuals with cancer reported not engaging in moderate physical aerobic activities on a weekly basis compared to 28% of those without, and three times as many of the former group reported being unable to engage in such activities (1.6% vs. 0.5%). Findings related to strength training revealed that close to 70% of those with a history of cancer and 60% of those without such diagnosis did not participate in strength exercises on a weekly basis. Three times as many cases compared to non-cases reported being unable to perform such activities (1.5% vs. 0.5%). The distribution of sleep patterns between those with cancer and those without differed such that a higher percentage of individuals with cancer slept ≤ 4 hours/night (3.3% vs. 2.8%) or ≥ 9 hours/night (13.0% vs. 8.5%). Approximately 89% of individuals without a history of cancer slept between 5 and 8 hours per night, compared to 84% of cancer cases. Table 3 presents the frequency with which cancer cases and non-cases engage in CAIM practices. Findings from this investigation indicate that the uptake of these practices is relatively low regardless of cancer status. Approximately 11% of both groups have seen or spoken with a chiropractor or massage therapist, and less than 3% have sought out services for acupuncture, naturopathy, and art or music therapy in the past year. Approximately 18% of the overall sample self-reported practicing meditation during the past 12 months, mainly for the purposes of restorative health. Table 3. CAIM characteristics of NHIS (2022) participants stratified by cancer status No cancer Cancer N = 24,184 N = 3430 χ 2 p -value Characteristic N (%) N (%) Chiropractor Seen/talked to 2649 (11.0) 382 (11.1) 0.104 0.748 Pain 2244 (9.3) 334 (9.7) 0.747 0.387 Restorative overall health 1921 (7.9) 278 (8.1) 0.107 0.743 Acupuncture Seen/talked to 556 (2.3) 108 (3.1) 9.241 0.002 Pain 416 (1.7) 86 (2.5) 10.428 0.001 Restorative overall health 398 (1.6) 79 (2.3) 7.650 0.006 Massage Seen/talked to 2753 (11.4) 380 (11.1) 0.278 0.598 Pain 1483 (6.1) 217 (6.3) 0.196 0.658 Restorative overall health 2106 (8.7) 319 (9.3) 1.314 0.252 Naturopathy Seen/talked to 342 (1.4) 51 (1.5) 0.113 0.736 Pain 173 (0.7) 26 (0.8) 0.760 0.782 Restorative overall health 288 (1.2) 47 (1.4) 0.807 0.369 Meditation Practice 4338 (17.9) 624 (18.2) 0.132 0.716 Pain 777 (3.2) 152 (4.4) 13.722 < 0.001 Restorative overall health 3195 (13.2) 470 (13.7) 0.630 0.427 Guided imagery/progressive muscle relaxation Practice 1600 (6.6) 253 (7.4) 2.773 0.096 Pain 385 (1.6) 78 (2.3) 8.478 0.004 Restorative overall health 1280 (5.3) 200 (5.8) 1.715 0.190 Yoga Practice 3865 (16.0) 425 (12.4) 29.520 < 0.001 Breathing 3222 (13.7) 355 (10.3) 29.846 < 0.001 Meditation 2266 (9.4) 210 (6.1) 38.809 < 0.001 Pain 1155 (4.8) 134 (3.9) 5.100 0.024 Restorative overall health 3093 (12.8) 364 (10.6) 13.002 < 0.001 Art therapist 110 (0.5) 20 (0.6) 1.054 0.304 Music therapist 151 (0.6) 30 (0.9) 2.889 0.089 Open in a new tab While there were no significant differences in utilization of chiropractic care, massage therapy, naturopathy services, or art or music therapy between cases and non-cases, individuals with cancer were more likely to have sought out acupuncture services for both pain ( p = 0.002) and restorative health ( p = 0.006). Likewise, although there were no significant differences in the practices of meditation, guided imagery, or progressive muscle relaxation for restorative overall health between the groups, cases were significantly more likely to use these practices to aid with pain relief ( p ≤ 0.005). Despite an increased likelihood of seeking out CAIM alternatives for pain, a smaller percentage of individuals with cancer self-reported practicing some form of yoga compared to those without a history of cancer (12.4% vs. 16.0%, p < 0.001). Additionally, non-cases were significantly more likely to engage in yoga-related breathing ( p < 0.001) and meditation ( p < 0.001) sessions, and approximately 5% engaged in these activities for the purpose of pain relief compared to 4% of cases ( p = 0.024). Discussion Findings from this investigation indicate that utilization of CAIM practices in the US is relatively low compared to non-Western regions of the world [ 5 ]. Approximately 18% of adults reported meditating in the past year, 15% engaged in some form of yoga, and 11% sought out chiropractic and/or massage services. Comparing the utilization of CAIM therapies between people with and without cancer can inform healthcare providers of the different needs, health practices, preferences, and types of CAIM adopted. While individuals with cancer were significantly more likely than those without to participate in acupuncture, meditation, guided imagery, and progressive muscle relaxation activities for the purposes of pain relief, the utilization of these practices was < 5% in both groups. Additionally, individuals without a history of cancer were more likely to practice yoga than those with a diagnosis of cancer, despite a growing evidence base indicating that yoga may be beneficial for overall mental and physical health. Understanding the motivations, barriers, and facilitators behind adopting CAIM practices is as equally important as the health benefits and is an area of needed research in the oncology population. Even though the literature exploring the therapeutic benefit of CAIM practices in oncology is expanding, many of the published studies are primarily among women with breast cancer [ 14 , 24 ]. Moreover, the heterogeneity of these practices, as well as their timing and duration of delivery during the trajectory of treatment and survivorship, together with various symptom targets, make it challenging to conclude outcome benefits and generalize findings among diverse oncology patient populations [ 20 ]. In addition, it remains unclear whether yoga, one of the most commonly researched CAIM modalities, is more or less effective than other exercise programs [ 12 ]. Furthermore, studies include several different yoga practices with varying degrees of movement, breathwork, and mindfulness components, making it difficult to differentiate which aspects of the practice might be responsible for the reported outcomes. In addition to yoga, the impact of mindfulness-based interventions (MBIs) has been studied to mitigate cancer and treatment-related symptoms such as anxiety, fatigue, depression, stress, quality of life, pain, cognitive function, and sleep. Findings from a recent systematic review and meta-analysis of 29 randomized controlled trials (RCTs) with a participant sample of N = 3479 reported that oncology patients who received MBIs had significantly lower anxiety, depression, fatigue, and stress along with improved quality of life compared to controls [ 25 ]. Research also indicates that mindfulness has an impact on the physiological markers of stress [ 26 ]. Other research reported no benefit of a mindfulness-based stress reduction (MBSR) program on neuropathic pain [ 27 ] and only subjective but not objective benefits for cognitive function among women with breast cancer [ 28 ]. Likewise, a systematic review of 22 RCTs evaluating physical and mind–body practices on sleep indicated that these activities provided some benefit for sleep quality but no significant improvement for other sleep parameters [ 29 ]. Once again, a majority of studies exploring MBIs are in breast cancer [ 25 ]. The interventions used in these studies were heterogeneous in content, duration, and scope, thereby making definitive conclusions about the impact of such practices (particularly among men and individuals with other types of cancer) difficult to quantify. Studies related to the effect of MBIs among patients diagnosed with other cancer types also indicate that mind–body interventions may reduce pain [ 30 ], decrease stress and anxiety [ 31 ], improve psychological and physical health [ 32 ], and improve sleep [ 33 ]. However, the majority of these findings, although efficacious, were small in magnitude. Similar to MBIs, investigations related to the impact of yoga in populations other than those with breast cancer have generally reported a positive effect on quality of life and physical/mental symptoms experienced by patients with cancer [ 12 , 34 , 35 ]. However, the degree of impact and intervention approaches varied widely. Nevertheless, despite a growing body of evidence demonstrating positive effects from these CAIM practices, the mechanisms by which such benefits are derived remain unclear. Furthermore, studies to date have been methodologically diverse with regard to patient participation, intervention strategies, outcome measures, and other factors [ 36 , 37 ]. Cancer-related fatigue is a pervasive and major symptom complaint among individuals with cancer before, during, and after treatment [ 15 , 16 ]. Findings from the present investigation indicate that patients with a self-reported history of any cancer type were significantly more likely to be tired on most days or every day of the week and significantly less likely to get the recommended 5–8 hours of sleep per night compared to individuals without cancer. Additionally, the former group was less likely to engage in weekly aerobic and strength training activities, as well as traditional yoga practices, potentially due to decreased mobility, increased pain, increased fatigue, reduced feelings of overall wellness, or a variety of other symptoms typically experienced by patients with cancer. These limiting factors may be real or perceived due to physical restrictions from surgery, tumor location, bony lesions, or other reasons, and such barriers warrant further research to explore additional modalities that could provide safe and effective intervention targets to increase uptake for clinical benefit guided by risk stratification models. While moderate or different types of exercise may pose physical challenges for cancer survivors, oncology-informed yoga offers a newer practice designed to consider disease-limiting factors for a prescribed yoga regimen that is unique to individual needs [ 38 ]. Oncology-informed yoga may provide a safer and gentler option to improve mobility, flexibility, strength, and stress relief. Future studies are needed to elucidate the reasons why cancer survivors are less likely to engage in traditional yoga practices and to evaluate whether an oncology-informed yoga program may offer an option for individuals who are not able to participate in standard yoga practices. Despite the growing evidence base supporting the potentially positive impact of CAIM practices on overall well-being, this study’s findings indicate the current limited engagement in these practices in the US. Therefore, studies to date must be interpreted with caution, as they are likely to be subject to participation biases. This is evidenced by a recent study related to mindfulness among patients recovering from colorectal cancer in which the uptake among eligible cases was 28%, and the dropout rate was 34% [ 39 ]. Selection biases, relatively small sample sizes, and a lack of standardized interventions across studies have made it difficult to accurately assess the true influence and magnitude of these practices. The lack of adoption of CAIM practices into mainstream medicine in the US may be due to a variety of patient and provider factors, including (but not limited to) lack of knowledge regarding the practices, lack of confidence to perform the activities, and cultural, structural, or financial barriers. A review by Jones et al. (2019) [ 20 ] identified the main contributing factors influencing CAIM use in individuals with cancer as disease status and socioeconomic factors, attitudes and beliefs, culture, health literacy, and healthcare access. From the healthcare provider’s perspective, the lack of uptake for CAIM integration in practice may be due to inconsistent study findings regarding therapeutic benefits or the magnitude of the impact of particular approaches on patient outcomes. Likewise, healthcare providers may have concerns regarding practices that interfere with the curative treatment intent of conventional regimens, such that patient delay or avoidance of timely treatment can negatively impact treatment outcomes [ 40 ]. These concerns regarding the appropriateness of CAIM therapies and integration of care are evident in a recent study in Saudi Arabia exploring trends in CAIM use among a cohort of individuals with cancer in 2006 compared to 2018 ( N = 1416), which found a significant use change [ 22 ]. CAIM was more likely used as an alternative treatment for cancer in the 2006 cohort, whereas in the 2018 cohort, CAIM was used for symptom management. CAIM use was less for the 2018 cohort, and there was more physician opposition to CAIM use compared to 2006. A recent national study in the US reported 33.3% of those with a history of cancer using CAIM [ 21 ]. In Australia, Keene et al. (2022) [ 9 ] reported CAIM use of 40.2%, and the primary reasons were to mitigate symptoms of cancer, side effects of treatment, and to improve overall health. However, CAIM users were more likely to have used it before their cancer diagnosis. The main reasons reported for non-CAIM use were lack of knowledge and fear of interactions with cancer treatments. Studies exploring CAIM use among individuals with cancer in the US indicate that many health providers do not have confidence in these approaches because of the limited and low level of evidence to support their use, lack of standardization of practices, and concern for adverse effects or interaction with conventional medicine [ 20 , 21 ]. Similar healthcare provider attitudes were identified in studies out of Australia, finding that in addition to concerns regarding inconsistent scientific evidence, healthcare providers have concerns regarding the safety and regulation of the heterogeneity of CAIM practices as well as financial costs [ 9 , 41 ]. The recent development of clinical practice guidelines for these practices in the management of cancer-related fatigue [ 42 , 43 ], as well as anxiety and depression [ 11 ], is available; however, the uptake and impact on patient outcomes in the use of these guidelines have yet to be realized. The limited uptake of CAIM practices in the US requires further research to identify barriers at the health system, provider, and patient levels. Furthermore, Hispanic, Black, and Asian populations with a history of cancer were under-represented in this data set; therefore, it is difficult to draw conclusions regarding the impact of ethnicity and race on the uptake of CAIM practices. Well-designed, large-scale RCTs with diverse representation of ethnicity and race are needed to fully elucidate the impact of these CAIM practices on the psychological and physiological health outcomes of cancer survivors. Most importantly, our understanding of the biological pathways involved in CAIM practices and the potential beneficial epigenetic influences of CAIM through gene regulation is limited for those with non-cancer and cancer alike [ 44 ]. There is emerging evidence that practices such as yoga and meditation may influence gene expression through epigenetic modifications by down-regulating pro-inflammatory cytokines and enhancing natural defense genes in the general population [ 45 ]. However, further research is needed to more fully understand the therapeutic effects of these practices and their biological and epigenetic influences on the population of cancer survivors. Limitations Although this study is strengthened by data collected from a large number of participants in a nationally representative sample, several limitations must be considered. First, the NHIS questionnaire solicited responses to general questions about meditation, yoga, and other activities; however, these disciplines encompass a wide variety of practices that were not specifically explored. Additionally, definitions of these practices may be somewhat subjective and can vary from individual to individual. Likewise, data related to the frequency and duration of participation in the activities were thereby unavailable, making it difficult to assess the extent of the practice. A second limitation is the lack of data among the individuals with cancer regarding their date of diagnosis, type of cancer, duration of disease, disease severity, staging, disease status, treatment modality, and complications, pain status, and other comorbid conditions and variables that might influence one’s decision and/or ability to participate in complementary medicine practices. Thirdly, the findings may be subject to participation and reporting biases, although the direction of such impact is unclear. While several CAIM interventions have shown promise for improving a variety of quality of life outcomes among individuals with cancer, it remains unclear which practices are most beneficial for each of the emotional and physical symptoms experienced. It is likely that optimal practices may vary depending on a number of factors, including (but not limited to) tumor type, time in the trajectory of the illness (e.g., before, during, or after treatment), and an array of patients’ psychological and physical sequelae. Future research is necessary to evaluate the individual and combined effects of mindfulness and movement on the overall well-being of patients with cancer and to rigorously compare modalities to determine those that offer maximum relief for distressing cancer and cancer treatment-related symptoms. Additionally, the literature is sparse regarding how CAIM practices may impact the epigenetics of patients diagnosed and treated for cancer. Further research is needed to more fully understand how biological mechanisms are influenced by these practices. Conclusions Findings from this study indicate that fewer than 20% of US adults who completed the NHIS survey in 2022, which included individuals with cancer who have an increased burden of pain, reported participating in CAIM practices during the past 12 months. The use of CAIM therapies has demonstrated benefit for many individuals with and survivors of cancer; however, its utilization is minimal in these groups. As such, while some may not find CAIM therapies beneficial or of interest, providing education to this population has the potential to better aid in symptom management. Additionally, research is necessary to investigate influencing factors and barriers related to the limited uptake of these practices in the US, particularly for individuals with cancer, at the health system, provider, and patient levels, that are representative of ethnic and racial diversity. Large-scale clinical trials are necessary to better elucidate the impact of these particular CAIM activities on quality of life in cancer survivors and discover the biological mechanisms and epigenetic effects that may be responsible for improving health outcomes. Lastly, in the present study, individuals without a personal history of cancer were significantly more likely to practice yoga, which represents a finding that also requires further exploration. Acknowledgements The authors would like to thank the following individuals for their time, assistance, and guidance on this project: Patrick Dineen, Pamela Ginex, Clare Moran, Anne Moyer, Chitra Thakur, and Ioana Zervos. Author contributions B.N. collaborated on the conception of the manuscript, conducted the analyses and prepared the first draft of the manuscript. S.W. made significant contributions to the manuscript by providing clinical expertise, an up to date literature review and information on current practices. S.W. reviewed the analyses and was instrumental is shaping the Discussion section of this work. L.M. collaborated on the conception of the manuscript, reviewed the analyses and helped to formulate the overall focus of the manuscript. All authors reviewed the manuscript. Declarations Conflict of interest The authors have no relevant financial or non-financial interests to disclose. The authors declare no competing interests. Ethics approval This study was exempt from the Stony Brook research ethics committee, as the study utilized a publicly available, de-identified dataset. Informed consent Not required. Consent to publish Not required. Footnotes Publisher''s Note Springer Nature remains neutral with regard to jurisdictional claims in published maps and institutional affiliations. References 1. Siegel RL, Giaquinto AN, Jemal A (2024) Cancer statistics, 2024. CA Cancer J Clin 74:12–49 [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Nekhlyudov L, Mollica MA, Jacobsen PB et al (2019) Developing a quality of cancer survivorship care framework: implications for clinical care, research, and policy. JNCI J Natl Cancer Inst 111:1120–1130 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Complementary and Alternative Medicine. National Cancer Institute. https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/cam . Accessed 6 June 2025 4. Ng JY, Dhawan T, Fajardo R-G et al (2023) The brief history of complementary, alternative, and integrative medicine terminology and the development and creation of an operational definition. Integr Med Res 12:100978 [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 5. Phutra