l multicentre study of oncology professionals in Norway. Eur J Cancer. 2004;40:529-535. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Newell S, Sanson-Fisher RW. Australian oncologists’ self-reported knowledge and attitudes about non-traditional therapies used by cancer patients. Med J Aust. 2000;172:110-113. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 25. Kim DY, Kim B-S, Lee KH, et al. Discrepant views of Korean medical oncologists and cancer patients on complementary and alternative medicine. Cancer Res Treat. 2008;40:87-92. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 26. Richardson MA, Mâsse LC, Nanny K, Sanders C. Discrepant views of oncologists and cancer patients on complementary/alternative medicine. Support Care Cancer. 2004;12:797-804. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Salmenperä L, Suominen T, Vertio H. Physicians’ attitudes towards the use of complementary therapies (CTs) by cancer patients in Finland. Eur J Cancer Care (Engl). 2003;12:358-364. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Tasaki K, Maskarinec G, Shumay DM, Tatsumura Y, Kakai H. Communication between physicians and cancer patients about complementary and alternative medicine: exploring patients’ perspectives. Psychooncology. 2002;11:212-220. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 29. Ben-Arye E, Halabi I, Attias S, Goldstein L, Schiff E. Asking patients the right questions about herbal and dietary supplements: cross cultural perspectives. Complement Ther Med. 2014;22:304-310. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials Supplement_1_Indicator_variables_LCA – Supplemental material for Information and Training Needs Regarding Complementary and Alternative Medicine: A Cross-sectional Study of Cancer Care Providers in Germany Click here for additional data file. (27.1KB, docx) Supplemental material, Supplement_1_Indicator_variables_LCA 